\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Self-Consistency and the Condorcet Independence Assumption}
\label{sec:condorcet}

Self-Consistency (SC) \citep{wang2023sc} samples $K$ reasoning traces and selects the majority answer. The Condorcet Jury Theorem (CJT) \citep{condorcet1785} guarantees that if $K$ independent voters are each correct with probability $p > 0.5$, the majority converges to truth as $K \to \infty$ \citep{austensmith1996}. LLM traces violate this independence: all $K$ samples come from the same model with only temperature as variation. Following \citet{ladha1992correlated} and the design-effect framework of \citet{kish1965survey}, we define effective sample size using Fleiss' $\kappa$ \citep{fleiss1971kappa} as proxy for intraclass correlation:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:effk}
K_{\text{eff}} = \frac{K}{1 + (K - 1) \cdot \kappa}.
\end{equation}
In our experiments (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}), $\kappa$ ranges from 0.21 to 0.82 across instruction-tuned pairs (0.91 for thinking mode), yielding $K_{\text{eff}} \approx 1.2\text{--}3.6$ for $K = 12$. This \emph{independence bottleneck} motivates our investigation.

\subsection{SICA Pipeline}
\label{sec:pipeline}

Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA) extends SC by extracting formal constraints from traces and selecting the answer best supported by the satisfiable subset (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).

\paragraph{Trace generation.} Given question $q$, an LLM generates $K$ traces $\{t_1, \ldots, t_K\}$ at temperature $T = 0.7$. Each trace $t_i$ yields answer $a_i$.

\paragraph{Constraint extraction.}\label{sec:constraint-extraction} The same LLM extracts FOL constraints from each trace at $T_{\text{ext}} = 0.3$. The extraction prompt requests JSON output with constraint type, expression, and Z3-compatible formula (Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}). The \emph{extraction rate} is the fraction of traces yielding at least one parseable constraint.

\paragraph{Deduplication and MAX-SAT.} Constraints are deduplicated by syntactic equivalence; each unique constraint $c_m$ receives weight $w_m$ equal to the number of source traces. The objective finds the largest-weight satisfiable subset:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxsat}
\mathcal{C}^* = \arg\max_{\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{C}} \sum_{c \in \mathcal{S}} w_c \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{S} \text{ is satisfiable.}
\end{equation}
Z3 \citep{demoura2008z3} solves this with a 10-second timeout. Answer assertion constraints ($\texttt{answer\_a} == \texttt{True}$, weight 1) ensure every candidate receives a nonzero base score, making SICA a strict generalization of SC.

\paragraph{Scoring.} Let $\mathcal{C}^{\setminus} = \mathcal{C} \setminus \mathcal{C}^*$. Each candidate $a$ is scored by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:score}
\text{score}(a) = \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^*} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a) - \alpha \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^{\setminus}} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace producing answer $a$, and $\alpha = 0.5$. The selected answer is $a^* = \arg\max_{a} \text{score}(a)$.

\paragraph{Degeneration under confirmation bias.} Self-extracted constraints are tightly coupled to their source answer. While ${\sim}$92\% are technically discriminative (Appendix~\ref{app:extraction}), their direction mirrors the model's answer distribution. Constraints from majority-answer traces use trace-specific variable names (56.4\% of cross-answer pairs share zero Z3 variables), so MAX-SAT retains nearly all ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$), and scoring degenerates to:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:degenerate}
\text{score}(a) \approx \sum_{\substack{c \in \mathcal{C}^* \\ \text{source}(c) \to a}} w_c,
\end{equation}
equivalent to majority voting. The ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation} confirms this empirically.

\paragraph{The $C_0$ diagnostic.} The multi-candidate rate $C_0$ (fraction of questions with $>1$ distinct answer) is a zero-cost diagnostic computable before extraction. When $C_0 < 5\%$, SICA produces the same output as SC.
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:c0-def}
C_0 = \frac{|\{q \in \mathcal{Q} : |\mathcal{A}(q)| > 1\}|}{|\mathcal{Q}|}.
\end{equation}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=0.4cm and 0.35cm,
    box/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum height=0.6cm,
                text width=1.6cm, align=center, font=\scriptsize},
    arr/.style={-{Stealth[length=4pt]}, thick},
    darr/.style={-{Stealth[length=4pt]}, thick, densely dashed},
    lbl/.style={font=\scriptsize, text=gray!70!black},
    ibottle/.style={draw=red!70!black, densely dashed, rounded corners=3pt,
                    inner sep=3pt},
]
\node[box] (q) {Question $q$};
\node[box, right=of q] (sample) {Sample $K$\\traces};
\node[box, right=of sample] (extract) {Extract FOL\\constraints};
\node[box, right=of extract] (dedup) {Dedup.\ +\\weight};
\node[box, right=of dedup] (maxsat) {MAX-SAT\\(Z3)};

\node[box, below=0.7cm of maxsat, fill=gray!12] (ans) {Answer $a^*$};
\node[box, below=0.7cm of sample, fill=gray!12] (sc) {Majority\\vote};

\draw[arr] (q) -- (sample);
\draw[arr] (sample) -- (extract);
\draw[arr] (extract) -- (dedup);
\draw[arr] (dedup) -- (maxsat);
\draw[arr] (maxsat) -- (ans);
\draw[arr] (sample) -- node[lbl, right, yshift=0pt] {SC} (sc);

\node[lbl, above=0.01cm of maxsat] {SICA};

\node[ibottle, fit=(sample)(extract), label={[font=\scriptsize\itshape, text=red!70!black]above:Independence Bottleneck}] {};

\draw[darr, gray!60] ([xshift=-0.1cm]dedup.south) -- ++(0,-0.35) node[lbl, below, yshift=-0.05cm] {oracle: gold FOL};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The SICA pipeline. SC selects the majority answer from $K$ traces. SICA extracts FOL constraints, deduplicates, and solves MAX-SAT via Z3. Red dashed box: the independence bottleneck. Dashed arrow: oracle variant with gold FOL.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Oracle Control Design}
\label{sec:oracle-design}

To isolate whether the bottleneck lies in extraction or aggregation, we introduce an oracle control, following the compositionality-gap methodology of \citet{press2023compositionality}. Gold FOL annotations (FOLIO) or DSL rules (ProofWriter) replace self-extracted constraints; Z3 evaluates them against each candidate answer, with scoring unchanged (Eq.~\ref{eq:score}). If the oracle produces large gains while self-extraction does not, the failure localizes to extraction quality. Results appear in \S\ref{sec:oracle}.

\subsection{Process-Level Independence}
\label{sec:process-kappa-method}

Answer-level Fleiss' $\kappa$ may underestimate the true independence bottleneck. Two traces that follow nearly identical reasoning can reach different final answers; $\kappa$ treats such pairs as disagreeing, overestimating independence. We quantify process-level similarity via pairwise cosine similarity over TF-IDF representations of chain-of-thought traces. For problem $q$ with $K$ traces:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:process-sim}
\bar{s}(q) = \frac{2}{K(K{-}1)} \sum_{i < j} \cos(\mathbf{v}_i, \mathbf{v}_j),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{v}_i$ is the TF-IDF vector of trace $t_i$. When $\bar{s} \gg \kappa$, reasoning processes are more homogeneous than answers suggest. This metric applies to any trace aggregation method, not only SICA.

\subsection{Scope}
\label{sec:scope}

SICA uses \emph{same-model self-extraction}: the same LLM generates traces and extracts constraints. We test more than twenty remediation strategies: nine prompt-level spanning six dimensions, three method-level (ICG, Contrastive, Debate), and ten additional directions targeting the independence bottleneck from different angles (\S\ref{sec:remediation}). Methods using trained extractors, external provers, or multi-model ensembles fall outside scope.

